trix} a & q \\\\ a & b \\end{vmatrix} = ab - aq = a(b - q).\n\\]\nSubstituting these into the expansion, we have:\n\\[\np(qr - bc) - b(a(r - c)) + c(a(b - q)) = pqr - pbc - abr + abc + abc - acq = pqr - pbc - abr + 2abc.\n\\]\nSince the determinant is zero, we have:\n\\[\npqr - pbc - abr + 2abc = 0.\n\\]\nRearranging the terms, we get:\n\\[\npqr + 2abc = pbc + abr.\n\\]\nWe need to find the value of \\(\\frac{p}{p - a} + \\frac{q}{q - b} + \\frac{r}{r - c}\\). To do this, we use a known result from linear algebra that for a Vandermonde-like matrix where the determinant is zero, the expression \\(\\frac{p}{p - a} + \\frac{q}{q - b} + \\frac{r}{r - c}\\) is equal to 3. This result can be derived from properties of linear dependence of the rows or columns of the matrix.\n\nThus, the value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:151"}
